  {
   "tab": "Gas pipelines",
   "row": 4631,
   "column": "ResearcherNotes",
   "cell": "T4631",
   "value": "Kinder Morgan's third quarter 2026 earnings release, filed with the SEC on October 21, 2026, says Southern Natural Gas has made a final investment decision on the Southeast Connector Project. It will carry up to 2.6 billion cubic feet a day through about 470 miles of new pipeline, with service starting in phases from the fourth quarter of 2030. An Oil and Gas Journal article from October 2026 reports the same figures. Southern Natural Gas announced a binding open season on September 16, 2026, which closed October 7, 2026. That notice names a new supply line, the SEC Extension, taking gas in Tennessee from Tennessee Gas Pipeline, Columbia Gulf, Texas Eastern and Midwestern Gas Transmission. It also expands the Southern Natural Gas system in Tennessee, Alabama, Georgia, South Carolina and Florida, and gives a target in-service date of November 1, 2030, with later phases no later than November 1, 2032. A WPLN News article from October 28, 2026 says 150 of the 470 miles are in Tennessee, the pipe is 48 inches wide, and capacity could grow to 3.5 billion cubic feet a day. The diameter comes from that article alone. Kinder Morgan put its own share of the cost at 3.1 billion US dollars. The full project cost was not found, so no cost is entered. The end point of the new line was not found in any source, so the end location is left blank. Status stays proposed because construction has not started and no federal permit application was found. This is a separate project from South System Expansion 4 (P7855) and from Transco's Southeast Supply Enhancement Project (P7108).",
   "kind": "value",
   "pid": "P8377"
  },
  {
